  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 500 AM MDT Tue Sep 22 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nLarge scale ridging centered east of the region will maintain above \naverage warmth and a southwesterly flow aloft across the Northern \nRockies and MT through Friday before a broadening upper trough in \nthe North Pacific edges inland this weekend. An upper level \ndisturbance and some monsoon-related moisture embedded within the \nsouthwesterly flow around the ridge will spread increasing cloud-\ncover northward across the area today with scattered showers and \nisolated weak thunderstorms tracking across primarily SW MT late \nthis afternoon through tonight. The warmest temperatures this week\nare likely Thursday and Friday as southwesterly flow increases \nahead of the incoming upper trough with afternoon temperatures \npushing well into the 80s for many areas. This would be around 15 \ndegrees above seasonal averages but well below daily records, which\nare still in the lower to mid 90s. \n\nA relatively strong Pacific cold front is forecast to traverse the \nnorthern Rockies late Friday into Friday night. However, model \ndiscrepancies persist regarding the precise timing and intensity of \nthe embedded shortwave as it lifts northeastward from the Northern \nRockies into AB by Saturday morning. Gusty southwest winds are \nlikely to precede the front late Friday with showers and potentially\nsome thunderstorms accompanying the frontal passage, followed by \ngusty west winds and cooler temperatures for the weekend. \n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nInstability will be constrained this afternoon due to high cloud-\ncover and decreasing late September solar input, keeping CAPE values \nmostly under 500 J/kg. Consequently, thunderstorm threats will be\nlimited primarily to isolated lighting strikes and wind gusts to \naround 30 mph. \n\nThe main hazard to watch with the approaching Pacific trough and cold \nfront early this weekend remains the potential for elevated winds \nFriday night into Saturday. Recent model guidance has shifted the \nshortwave slightly further north however, and somewhat weakened \nthe accompanying mid-level wind field. The overall setup, featuring\ndownward transport of winds following the shortwave and moderate \npressure rises in the wake of the front, continues to support the\npotential for locally strong winds gusts and probabilistic guidance\nnow indicates a 30-40% chance of gusts exceeding 55 mph along the\nRocky Mountain Front and adjacent areas on Saturday. Hoenisch",
